:: R1_Office_Day2 [r1 story]
<<location "Meridian Consulting, Floor 39">>

Meridian is the same building doing the same things with the same people inside it. The only difference is the day printed on Greg Tanaka's calendar and the flavor of coffee in the machine. Yesterday was "Bold Roast." Today is "Smooth Morning." Both taste like hot brown disappointment.

I work through the Henderson prep. Three scenarios: baseline, bullish, regulatory headwind. I build slides with the precision of a man constructing a bomb shelter, every data point reinforced, every assumption cited. If something goes wrong Thursday, it won't be because I wasn't ready. That's my brand. Not "brilliant." Not "creative." Ready. Prepared. The guy who triple-checks.

At 11 AM I pass Harper in the break room. She's eating an orange, peeling it in one continuous spiral, the way some people can do and I absolutely cannot.

<<sayself>>Talent.<</sayself>>

I say it, nodding at the peel.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>Practice. I eat an orange every day. Takes about three weeks to learn the one-strip technique.<</say>>

She bites a section. Juice on her chin. She wipes it with the back of her hand.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>You're over-indexing on Henderson.<</say>>

<<sayself>>What?<</sayself>>

<<say "Harper Quinn">>I can see it on your face. You've done the work. Stop revising. You'll start second-guessing the good stuff if you keep touching it.<</say>>

She tosses the peel in the compost bin, a small gesture of environmental optimism in a building that burns enough electricity to power a small town.

<<say "Harper Quinn">>Trust the prep.<</say>>

She walks out. I stand there holding my mug of Smooth Morning, which has gone tepid, and I think about the fact that Harper Quinn, who is five years younger than me and two rungs lower on the org chart, just told me to relax, and she was right, and the fact that she was right bothers me less than the fact that she could tell.

