:: R1_Closing_Evening [r1 story]
<<set $time = { day: "thursday", period: "evening", week: 1 }>>
<<location "Multiple, Rest of the Week">>

Wednesday and Thursday pass in a smear of meetings and decks and lunches I don't taste. The Henderson meeting is Thursday and it goes well. Not brilliantly. Not catastrophically. The client nods. Greg Tanaka nods. Martin Crane doesn't attend, which either means he trusts me or he doesn't consider it worth his time, and I choose to believe the former because the latter would keep me up at night.

Thursday evening. I get home at 6:40. Lena is on the couch in yoga pants, laptop open, something for her freelance graphic design work. She's good at it. Better than she gives herself credit for. When we met, she talked about starting her own studio. Now she takes small contracts and talks about it less.

<<sayself>>Pizza or Thai?<</sayself>>

<<say "Lena">>Thai. Get the pad see ew.<</say>>

<<sayself>>Same as always?<</sayself>>

She glances up. Holds my gaze for a half-second longer than usual. There's something there, some flicker behind the warmth of her brown eyes. Not dissatisfaction, exactly. Something quieter. A question she's not asking.

<<say "Lena">>Same as always.<</say>>

She says it, and looks back at her screen.

I order the Thai. We eat on the couch. Her feet in my lap. I rub her arches absentmindedly while she eats and scrolls and occasionally shows me a design mockup for a local bakery's website. The lavender is too muted, I say. She squints at the screen, says maybe, changes it to something slightly brighter. This is us at our best: functional, cooperative, two people sharing a couch and a pad see ew and the comfortable lie that this is enough.

